<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Imagawa clan</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Imagawa were a Japanese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a> clan, a branch of the Ashikaga founded in the 13th century. From the 14th century they served as shugo governors of Suruga and later Tōtōmi, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sengoku-period">Sengoku period</a> they became powerful <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daimyo">daimyo</a> ruling from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunpu">Sunpu</a>. Their power peaked under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imagawa-yoshimoto">Imagawa Yoshimoto</a> but collapsed after his death at Okehazama in 1560, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/takeda-shingen">Takeda Shingen</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a> divided their lands by 1569.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/imagawa-clan.jpg" alt="Imagawa clan" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mukai · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Japanese_crest_Imagawa_Akadori.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
